<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Graham Bell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander Graham Bell was a Scottish-born scientist and inventor credited with patenting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> in 1876. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> met him at the Centennial Exhibition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> that year and was impressed by the device. The encounter is often cited as an example of Pedro II's interest in science and modern technology.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-graham-bell.jpg" alt="Alexander Graham Bell" width="283" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Graham_Bell_1895_NPG_77_363.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pedro II was well suited to the role by temperament and intellect: genuinely learned, interested in science and philosophy (he corresponded with Louis Pasteur and Henry Wadsworth Longfellow and met <b>Alexander Graham Bell</b>), committed to appointment on merit, and personally opposed to slavery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the first piece transmitted by telephone, at <b>Alexander Graham Bell</b>'s first demonstration before the American Academy of Arts and Sciences in Boston on 10 May 1876.</blockquote><a href="/songs/old-100th">Old 100th</a></li><li><blockquote>Companies holding the telephone patents of Thomas Edison and <b>Alexander Graham Bell</b> combined to form the Oriental Telephone Company.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-25">25 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In his Boston laboratory <b>Alexander Graham Bell</b> spoke into his transmitter: 'Mr Watson, come here, I want to see you.' His assistant Thomas Watson heard him in the next room.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-10">10 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Beinn Bhreagh, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.19&amp;year=1895" aria-label="Edinburgh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4481 258 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexander Graham Bell"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 528h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4931 528h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.19&amp;year=1895">What was here in 1895? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
